#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198075 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/id #uuid "89942534-6b03-52ec-8494-830de5eb8128" :question/math-academy-id "e-12558" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](images/b0/b0a31426fa8f1d55dcc4a94fb7b6544cdba753d879a4b95c67b66662ce43a26e.svg)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y=-1+1=0 \\\\ z=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198228 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/id #uuid "5524aeab-cc64-5da8-bea3-8cd5dd86ebcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5264" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198229 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/id #uuid "b1f59935-cd61-5c59-aa8d-cf7ba7f151bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5618" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b975fd-e446-5a55-b271-ff62ed2f207d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=9{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483581} {:answer/id #uuid "563e7933-88b2-5d09-a466-e815264e47b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483723} {:answer/id #uuid "71de7fc3-0756-5137-bb21-59702b351d65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483792} {:answer/id #uuid "781c4a5e-6128-5f7c-8d4d-bc8159e4a2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483811} {:answer/id #uuid "e5de6f57-ca27-538b-8d1c-dacfab40980e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "781c4a5e-6128-5f7c-8d4d-bc8159e4a2de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483811} :answer-field/id #uuid "13a502e6-ea44-5bb6-a268-5801b7bef457" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75d5fcc2-1404-5925-8c53-e5c58b19a741" :question/math-academy-id "q-35928" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $6z-6={x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}$ and the plane $x+y=2$ onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=2\\,⟹\\,x=2-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-y)^{2}+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+3{y}^{2} & =6z-6 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{y}^{2}-4y+4 \\\\ 6z-6 & =4({y}^{2}-y+1) \\\\ 6z-6 & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+3 \\\\ 6z & =4{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+9 \\\\ z & =\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{2}{3}{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2376b38c-9ac7-5ccb-aef2-8febe77582e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483629} {:answer/id #uuid "4bdbf382-36e1-5d09-9697-08bf328751ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483706} {:answer/id #uuid "88e559ad-8691-5563-bef8-7c758497060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-1,3)$ and $(-2,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483850} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd52242-1eb0-5bc5-ba5f-f44479a5de55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(2,-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483940} {:answer/id #uuid "f793c3ab-6816-5e62-8e36-6ac1d69a8b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,1,3)$ and $(2,1,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbd52242-1eb0-5bc5-ba5f-f44479a5de55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,0)$ and $(2,-1,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483940} :answer-field/id #uuid "556152ba-ab4f-5902-a9cf-fccfb5e130ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01982d7c-8631-5513-adcd-2dc3bb87a98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70494" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=9$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t-3, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =9 \\\\ (t)^{2}+(t-3)^{2}+(t)^{2} & =9 \\\\ {t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-6t+9)+{t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-6t+9 & =9 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-6t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=(0)-3=-3 \\\\ z=(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-3,0).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(2)=2 \\\\ y=(2)-3=-1 \\\\ z=(2)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(2,-1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-3,0)$ and $(2,-1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ec7c4e7-0717-579f-af46-0ad1c775388d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483592} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8af2ed-6ade-5c05-8444-37e9f2e4600a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483751} {:answer/id #uuid "84d0a98a-843f-5cff-ab9b-d8f9b4d153b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483835} {:answer/id #uuid "f2c21e1a-675b-58de-a749-fcf9249347a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484055} {:answer/id #uuid "ff15e463-523f-56d5-be3c-4a291647e90e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f8af2ed-6ade-5c05-8444-37e9f2e4600a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483751} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38aa8a0-730c-5f61-9672-fbfd36ec5e9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f3934760-11ef-5a3e-86ba-585bb0d00514" :question/math-academy-id "q-70555" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=10$ and the plane $z-y=2$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,z=2+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(2+y)^{2} & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(4+4y+{y}^{2}) & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =10 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+1) & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y+1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096c9c95-3d61-58ff-a3ab-a51f804213af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483586} {:answer/id #uuid "3418e24d-c46a-5074-853c-a90a5378e76c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,1,-1)$ and $(3,-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483662} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c38b2-bd40-5fb8-9dcb-029898ecb5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,1)$ and $(-3,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483682} {:answer/id #uuid "7310d89c-868c-5739-8a05-2f80cc315acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483796} {:answer/id #uuid "df58b652-b996-5398-9e5e-cd540b98ba8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-1,-1)$ and $(-3,1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2c38b2-bd40-5fb8-9dcb-029898ecb5a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,1,1)$ and $(-3,-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483682} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f2384ba-6b35-5b35-a6c3-c24e2e8fdd94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3624fa42-c59d-5231-bc08-94f56f4ab26d" :question/math-academy-id "q-70570" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=1$ and the line $\\frac{x-3}{3}=y-1=z-1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{3}=y-1=z-1=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3t+3 \\\\ y=t+1 \\\\ z=t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{27}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+9{z}^{2} & =27 \\\\ (3t+3)^{2}+9(t+1)^{2}+9(t+1)^{2} & =27 \\\\ (9{t}^{2}+18t+9)+9({t}^{2}+2t+1)+9({t}^{2}+2t+1) & =27 \\\\ 27{t}^{2}+54t+27 & =27 \\\\ 27{t}^{2}+54t & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(0)+3=3 \\\\ y=(0)+1=1 \\\\ z=(0)+1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(3,1,1).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=3(-2)+3=-3 \\\\ y=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ z=(-2)+1=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-3,-1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(3,1,1)$ and $(-3,-1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20daa437-703f-5c1f-bfef-06c613eae5d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,6,-4)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483620} {:answer/id #uuid "68903205-36b5-5e27-95f6-4876748a4c2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,2,-6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483771} {:answer/id #uuid "822195d9-b4ca-5da4-a47f-e77424f2c224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483831} {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} {:answer/id #uuid "d1da9504-c5b6-53eb-a566-9e371e654451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-2,-4)$ and $(1,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9738f5d5-30f2-54f5-b519-04f2dece2bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483875} :answer-field/id #uuid "88c428fa-eb9a-5abc-94ad-cb031dd35e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7819f027-d28d-5660-b11c-cd0f95f720fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70957" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2}=36$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+1 \\\\ y=2t+2, \\\\ z=t+6\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {((t+1)-1)}^{2}+{((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(t+6)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+12t+36) & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t+36 & =36 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+12t & =0 \\\\ t(t+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)+1=1 \\\\ y=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ z=(0)+6=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,2,6).$\n\n- For $t=-2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-2)+1=-1 \\\\ y=2(-2)+2=-2 \\\\ z=(-2)+6=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,-2,4).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,2,6)$ and $(-1,-2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "338b118d-9bea-5bd0-994b-399c71333888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,3,-1)$ and $(-4,0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483659} {:answer/id #uuid "720db885-be87-54a4-99e5-45e7d112bf6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-4)$ and $(-1,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483794} {:answer/id #uuid "86bb3f55-b716-5227-b073-df61629436e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,2,-1)$ and $(0,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483845} {:answer/id #uuid "874b5494-11c0-50b9-8a3b-77cc02ec2985" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,0)$ and $(-4,-1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483847} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5dd78a-f677-56eb-95e4-42454a1b37a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-1)$ and $(-4,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e5dd78a-f677-56eb-95e4-42454a1b37a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-1)$ and $(-4,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483894} :answer-field/id #uuid "54918cdd-65dd-5ee0-a8b6-130e964c1af0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "925fed75-7149-58ca-b2ab-03e5b43ab0a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-70960" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+7)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=81$ and the line $-\\frac{x}{2}=2-y=\\frac{z+1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{2-y}{1}=\\frac{z+1}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t \\\\ y=2-t \\\\ z=2t-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (-2t)^{2}+((2-t)+7)^{2}+((2t-1)+1)^{2} & =81 \\\\ (-2t)^{2}+(9-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2} & =81 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+(81-18t+{t}^{2})+4{t}^{2} & =81 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}-18t+81 & =81 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}-18t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2(0)=0 \\\\ y=2-(0)=2 \\\\ z=2(0)-1=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,2,-1).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2(2)=-4 \\\\ y=2-(2)=0 \\\\ z=2(2)-1=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-4,0,3).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,2,-1)$ and $(-4,0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} {:answer/id #uuid "3402b0ae-2770-548b-bd7e-08caf8efcf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ and $(-2,3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483661} {:answer/id #uuid "476cbccb-7f02-581b-b415-12ad49fd0514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483697} {:answer/id #uuid "85f544ac-b2af-53d2-9846-ab93a0ca63da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483839} {:answer/id #uuid "85fe0a6f-92b1-5ed1-85c4-d3ebde706c06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,3,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19504466-0a10-574f-bb8c-3c8ded8f176c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483609} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f0fad20-662a-5ac9-aa37-fdaf4bd1c44b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bf7120f6-4fae-5d19-b842-ba88886669a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-71129" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t \\\\ y=2-t, \\\\ z=t-1\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+4(2-t)^{2}+2(t-1)^{2} & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4(4-4t+{t}^{2})+2({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =8 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+16-16t+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}-4t+2 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+18 & =8 \\\\ 10{t}^{2}-20t+10 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1 & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=1.$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)=2 \\\\ y=2-1=1 \\\\ z=1-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19af1e47-b0c8-5aa5-8bb9-46eef20e668a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(-1,-3,-9)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483610} {:answer/id #uuid "84798499-fed0-5ab8-b453-9364e7bc3bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(-3,-4,-10)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483834} {:answer/id #uuid "89e0e314-6093-5699-85bc-bf6985a0acec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{23}{3},-\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{14}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483853} {:answer/id #uuid "abc3bbc9-dc43-572e-9953-c02ca742e1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483914} {:answer/id #uuid "f1340dfa-8a97-5e37-b745-f69f2cf865bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{14}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abc3bbc9-dc43-572e-9953-c02ca742e1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483914} :answer-field/id #uuid "8159c22d-ec7f-5de6-bf20-9a2452034f66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54e46f90-207e-55ef-98ea-cffcdc18e2c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71960" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=100$ and the line $\\frac{x-5}{2}=-y=z+6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-5}{2}=\\frac{y}{-1}=\\frac{z+6}{1}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5 \\\\ y=-t \\\\ z=t-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ ((2t+5)+1)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+((t-6)-2)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (2t+6)^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(t-8)^{2} & =100 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+24t+36)+{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-16t+64) & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t+100 & =100 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+8t & =0 \\\\ 2t(3t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-\\frac{4}{3}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y=-(0)=0 \\\\ z=(0)-6=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,-6).$\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-\\frac{4}{3})+5=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ y=-(-\\frac{4}{3})=\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ z=(-\\frac{4}{3})-6=-\\frac{22}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,-6)$ and $(\\frac{7}{3},\\frac{4}{3},-\\frac{22}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484241 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16bd4c4d-53a0-526b-814f-cc329822f5c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}(y-4)^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483605} {:answer/id #uuid "210e1ce3-5722-50b6-b4c8-4929dd75f7d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2}+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483623} {:answer/id #uuid "5f664acd-0f58-51a7-829f-fb2b4bf1c158" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483750} {:answer/id #uuid "8db83346-df15-571a-88b3-9ec53b42513e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483862} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a7669c-5d9a-56be-933a-d77f93c3f227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}(y-4)^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8db83346-df15-571a-88b3-9ec53b42513e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483862} :answer-field/id #uuid "44cb9f95-73e3-524a-827d-1a98148e129a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e565e12-d691-5857-8d63-02efd0af7105" :question/math-academy-id "q-72337" :question/problem "Find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $z-4=(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}$ and the plane $x+y=-4$ onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx+y=-4\\,⟹\\,x=-4-y\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-4 & =((-4-y)-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =(-y-6)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & ={y}^{2}+12y+36+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{2}+12x+36 \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}({y}^{2}+8y+24) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(({y}^{2}+8y+16)+8) \\\\ z-4 & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+12 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the parabola $z=\\frac{3}{2}(y+4)^{2}+16.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a93ae61-117e-52eb-a561-05bda1830dfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483674} {:answer/id #uuid "3fa19c57-773f-538a-8210-ed0794e91c6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483688} {:answer/id #uuid "5a3c9888-3e12-5f64-b8ce-fec1d37e0fa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483736} {:answer/id #uuid "8eb0748f-88f4-5a20-a136-b18b07e72b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483864} {:answer/id #uuid "a1b4f9f0-5d69-54f2-a20f-cb95071368a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,1)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eb0748f-88f4-5a20-a136-b18b07e72b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,-4)$ in the $yz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483864} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf6552b0-6071-5cb6-8a4f-6d1b9d70cb89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f2db1db-7bfe-5b3b-ab6d-846d64109b8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-72342" :question/problem "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{5}x+4y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{4-4y}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(1-y)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5} & =z+4 \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y-1)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(1,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8caf6956-74b3-5656-bd1b-037cc3f8bd27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483858} {:answer/id #uuid "912fa47f-946c-5b4e-a27c-271bcf02a4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483867} {:answer/id #uuid "bd25a088-02c2-5d4e-87e7-de9467e51d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483942} {:answer/id #uuid "e75b5a59-d2cd-5446-ad00-5a6bf90899b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484025} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4a0b05-a807-5c98-96b4-150269a1c9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(3,-2,2)$ and $(-3,2,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd25a088-02c2-5d4e-87e7-de9467e51d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483942} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c2262e-fd4e-5f1d-8d6d-8e23cdf0a554" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83fca566-7995-55f0-a5c4-c64563aea218" :question/math-academy-id "q-73219" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3t \\\\ y=2t, \\\\ z=-2t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{12} & =1 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 4(-3t)^{2}+2(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2} & =12 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ 48{t}^{2} & =12 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z=-2(-\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z=-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{3}{2},-1,1)$ and $(-\\frac{3}{2},1,-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198349 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a426dc8c-2be0-5e83-93b6-54a634561aad" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Parametric Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34ef952d-86be-57c3-acf2-24a5e2ed5218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ and $(1,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483663} {:answer/id #uuid "5840fb0f-1558-5ec4-8955-40747a4b4779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,2,1)$ and $(1,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483728} {:answer/id #uuid "6e92db24-6441-5216-a2f0-a895392110bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(6,-5,8)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483785} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf06a63-9752-5e5d-8118-e92dea79d7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,0,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483819} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f6a4fd-f986-5139-9089-efd0086bbe54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4f6a4fd-f986-5139-9089-efd0086bbe54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,1,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484020} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ed6a25-4fb8-5bc4-ae1e-17b36f0b19cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d00df90-ee2d-5cb7-ace6-bf48f9f9acd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-73245" :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1$ and the line \n$\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=1-t, \\\\ z=t+2\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3(t)^{2}+2(1-t)^{2}+(t+2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2(1-2t+{t}^{2})+({t}^{2}+4t+4) & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2}+6 & =6 \\\\ 6{t}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solution $t=0$ back into the equations of the line.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=1-(0)=1 \\\\ z=(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the point is $(0,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,1,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1937aa4c-02a1-520f-b072-f81c55825fc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,2,2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483608} {:answer/id #uuid "381612bb-bf1f-5b5d-9e4f-e8deacf09159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2,-6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483669} {:answer/id #uuid "56d19d92-5983-5fc3-a97b-e1328867bdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483725} {:answer/id #uuid "74bc61bc-347a-5065-bb28-0f6dc479bcdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,4,6)$ and $(-2,0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483802} {:answer/id #uuid "a40e8f00-f264-53cd-9903-d88fc84da7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2,-6)$ and $(0,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40e8f00-f264-53cd-9903-d88fc84da7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,-2,-6)$ and $(0,2,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483905} :answer-field/id #uuid "89359ee7-4c2c-57c0-acaa-381ae6084719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "04b22284-5add-5373-b896-4f7022795490" :question/math-academy-id "q-94775" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x+1=y-1=\\frac{z}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx+1=y-1=\\frac{z}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t-1 \\\\ y=t+1 \\\\ z=2t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2(t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ 2({t}^{2}-2t+1)+({t}^{2}+2t+1) & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-4t+2+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+2t-3 & =0 \\\\ (t+3)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-3,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-3-1=-4 \\\\ y=-3+1=-2 \\\\ z=2(-3)=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-4,-2,-6).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(0,2,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-4,-2,-6)$ and $(0,2,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484195 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198433 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5329867-1956-5f36-8614-26d483c36efe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Surface and a Line Given in Cartesian Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "158e4055-a575-5687-b6c0-7b18540160c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1,0)$ and $(3,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483604} {:answer/id #uuid "9d644e50-890f-5bbd-af2c-3c7931490a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,-1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483887} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ce6c4c-4c00-5d76-8918-0c25150319f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,-6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483921} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e98c45-0e9a-57dc-a49d-771e71132536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,1,6)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483963} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c4ccfd-f5ea-5cea-a5ac-62d7e712889c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,-6)$ and $(4,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7c4ccfd-f5ea-5cea-a5ac-62d7e712889c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-3,-6)$ and $(4,1,6)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484068} :answer-field/id #uuid "101a7658-6522-5e95-9874-dab42cc55c4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03ac4047-3b67-5e2f-8026-813b5d163c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-94783" :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x-2=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\nx-2=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}=t\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t+2 \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=3t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2(t+2)^{2}+4(t-1)^{2} & =(3t)^{2} \\\\ 2({t}^{2}+4t+4)+4({t}^{2}-2t+1) & =9{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+8t+8+4{t}^{2}-8t+4 & =9{t}^{2} \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ 3(t+2)(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-2+2=0 \\\\ y=-2-1=-3 \\\\ z=3(-2)=-6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(0,-3,-6).$\n\n- For $t=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+2=4 \\\\ y=2-1=1 \\\\ z=3(2)=6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,1,6).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(0,-3,-6)$ and $(4,1,6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198354 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58427e6a-8e77-5c01-a578-a7dc4b8f8812" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Projection of the Intersection of a Surface and a Tilted Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "628b083b-64e7-5523-9a16-b836f553e8fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483755} {:answer/id #uuid "70cc3d86-a276-51ae-b8d9-d32a97d5be00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483789} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d182d3-ab5c-58a8-aa85-15afb61de658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola centered at $(-3,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484069} {:answer/id #uuid "fa27f81e-26da-55b9-b7c4-542c280c96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484073} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe86de5-9b39-5b64-9487-3456434b86bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa27f81e-26da-55b9-b7c4-542c280c96da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484073} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d014e84-90ae-55bd-8e44-eb912baf0075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58fab8e8-c40a-5f0c-89d7-e35fb797a4f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94954" :question/problem "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2(z-2)^{2}$ and the plane $2z-x-10=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-x-10=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{x}{2}+5\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+5-2)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2{(\\frac{x}{2}+3)}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =2(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+3x+9) \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+6x+18 \\\\ \\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}-6x+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}-4x+4)-6+(y+3)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{(x-2)}^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{{(x-2)}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{24} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of an ellipse centered at $(2,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane."}]}
